#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064458 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b492068c-2bd2-5611-baab-780959d24c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function Using a Table"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5110235-92c1-5a27-a99c-d71e2fe24850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061398} {:answer/id #uuid "ce568ea6-ae0b-5a67-87a9-94b73170df32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061399} {:answer/id #uuid "da1b6afb-a864-5662-b19c-7fa8166bccb0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061400} {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} {:answer/id #uuid "7c66de25-cfef-5e04-98e5-09487adc4a22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061402}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e627842-4f81-542b-a973-27f010a478f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061401} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc3f4f87-07a3-561b-a1e5-5bea819e76c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186061397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "34e33e76-2a83-5368-8561-def1a916040d" :question/math-academy-id "q-19201" :question/problem "The following table gives the values of $f(x),{f}^{′}(x),g(x),$ and ${g}^{′}(x)$ at some points. Find $({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)$ of the product of functions.\n\n| **$x$** | $f(x)$ | ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $g(x)$ | ${g}^{′}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $-3$ | $1$ |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $-2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of the inverse ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1})^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}.\n$$\n\nUsing this, and the product rule, the derivative of the function ${f}^{-1}(x)g(x)$ is\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(x)=\\frac{g(x)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(x))}+{g}^{′}(x){f}^{-1}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2)=\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2).\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we obtain ${f}^{-1}(2)=-1,$ $g(2)=-3,$ ${g}^{′}(2)=1,$ and ${f}^{′}(-1)=-2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({f}^{-1}\\cdot g)^{′}(2) & =\\frac{g(2)}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}+{g}^{′}(2){f}^{-1}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{{f}^{′}(-1)}+1\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{-2}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186322611 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "37e05385-7cb6-5f21-b0d8-a7641d912d5b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of an Inverse Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} {:answer/id #uuid "83ac79de-d929-59e9-b4cd-f03e4d332524" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5595ee00-3e08-5c85-ab82-a1dbb68af433" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322613} :answer-field/id #uuid "822f713b-c25e-5da8-a950-9db41fcefb5b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1f6eff9-e354-50e5-b0d6-7aed9742e28f" :question/math-academy-id "q-312579" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+2$ and the point $P(2,6)$ that lies on $y=f(x).$ Given that $f(x)$ is invertible in the vicinity of $P,$ calculate ${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6).$\n\n${({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We start by calculating the derivative of $f$ at the point $(2,6).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+2 \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =2x \\\\ {f}^{′}(2) & =4\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(2,6)$ lies on $y=f(x),$ we know that the corresponding point $(6,2)$ lies on $y={f}^{-1}(x).$\n\nFurthermore, the slopes of the tangents to the two respective points are reciprocals. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(6) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(6))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
